            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s **Perseverance Mars rover has completed a “marathon” on Mars**: it has driven **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** across the Red Planet. It reached that milestone on **Sol 1,890** of its mission, after about **five years and four months** of driving since landing in Jezero Crater in February 2021【5:0†source】.\n\nNASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter also captured an overhead image of Perseverance and its tracks on **June 13, 2026**, showing the rover as a tiny speck on the Martian surface just before the milestone was reached. At the time, Perseverance was operating west of Jezero Crater in a region the science team calls **“Arbot”**【5:0†source】.\n\nThe rover is still actively returning imagery: NASA’s Perseverance image archive lists recent “Image of the Week” selections, including **Week 284, July 19–25, 2026**, indicating the mission continues to collect and publish new surface images【5:2†source】.\n\nIn short: **Perseverance is healthy enough to keep exploring, has now driven a marathon distance on Mars, and continues sending back new images and science data.**",
